  cites Safari chapter Pose
  cites Safari chapter Rider
  cites Poc chapter Drive

 SPIKE ONLY. THROWAWAY. NOT PART OF THE PORT AND GRADED BY NOTHING.

 This chapter exists to put ONE still frame in front of a human, at a chosen point
 on the route, so a piece of scenery can be LOOKED AT instead of reasoned about.
 harness/spike_svg.py renders it to a flat SVG -- no wasm, no blitter, no
 animation -- because a picture you can open is a faster feedback loop than a page
 you have to drive to the right spot.

 Nothing here is verified. The graded port is `Safari chapter Render` and the
 checks in judge/; if this chapter and a check ever disagree, the CHECK is right.
 Delete this whole file the day it stops being useful and nothing else changes.

 WHAT IT ADDS TO A NORMAL FRAME IS NOW ONLY THE CAT. The animals draw themselves
 -- EmojiStills holds their art and Critter places it, both graded -- so a still
 here is what the browser draws, not an approximation of it. The cat's flipbook is
 a second table that is not generated yet, so it keeps a box, lifted by its hop so
 the leap still reads.

 We say:

Section: The Animals

 THE SPIKE STOPPED CHEATING FOR THESE. It used to emit a spike-only tag carrying a
 codepoint, and the SVG script read the game's baked table off disk to draw the
 animal -- because the port could not draw one at all. It can now: EmojiStills
 holds the art and Critter places it, graded at 5,916 coordinates against the real
 critter.draw. So the spike just calls the port, and the still shows exactly what
 the browser shows.

 The cat below still uses a tag, because its flipbook is a second table that is not
 generated yet.

  marker-of : Billboard, Real -> List DrawCmd
  marker-of (b) (cf) = critter-draw b.right b.fwd b.height b.cp b.face-right cf camera-w

  cat-marker-tag : Integer
  cat-marker-tag = 10

 THE CAT GETS ITS OWN SPIKE TAG because it is a FLIPBOOK, not a species: what has
 to travel is which still to draw, so the pose index rides in the colour word
 where the animals put their codepoint. cat_frames.zig turns out to be the same
 shape of table as emoji_frames -- named pose arrays of solid polygons in the same
 unit frame -- so the spike script reads it the same way.

 THE ANCHOR ALREADY CARRIES THE HOP. The feet are projected at the lift, not at
 the ground, so a mid-leap cat is drawn where it actually is in the air; and the
 height is measured from lifted feet to lifted ears, so it does not grow as it
 rises. That is what cat.zig's draw does with the same two numbers.

  cat-marker : CatItem, Real -> List DrawCmd
  cat-marker (c) (cf) =
    let base = project (Vec3 { right = c.right, forward = c.fwd, height = c.lift }) cf camera-w
    in let top = project (Vec3 { right = c.right, forward = c.fwd, height = c.lift + c.height }) cf camera-w
    in let h = base.y - top.y
    in if h < 1.0 then []
      else [DrawCmd { tag = cat-marker-tag, color = c.pose-idx, strength = 0.0, pts = [base.x, base.y, h] }]

Section: The Frame

 Drive's own dispatch with the two billboard cases filled in, so everything lands
 in the REAL depth order rather than being painted on top afterwards. That is the
 whole point: a pig behind a tree must be behind the tree.

  spike-item : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Collected, Item, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  spike-item (w) (ch) (pose) (c) (it) (cf) (step) =
   when it.kind
    is KCow -> marker-of (list-at c.cows it.i) cf
    is KCat -> cat-marker (list-at c.cats it.i) cf
    is KTree -> draw-one-tree c.trees it.i cf
    is KTower -> draw-one-tower w ch pose (list-at c.towers it.i) cf step
    is KRail -> rail-draw-poly (list-at c.rails it.i) cf camera-w
    is KTruck -> []

  spike-order : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Collected, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  spike-order (w) (ch) (pose) (c) (cf) (step) (i) =
    if i >= list-length c.order then []
    else (spike-item w ch pose c (list-at c.order i) cf step) & spike-order w ch pose c cf step (i + 1)

  spike-frame-at : Real -> List DrawCmd
  spike-frame-at (u) = spike-frame-in build-world u

  spike-frame-in : List Segment, Real -> List DrawCmd
  spike-frame-in (w) (u) =
    let p = drive-pos-at w u
    in let pose = pose-in w u
    in let step = scene-step-at u
    in let ch = build-chain w p.seg
    in (draw (heading-in w u) (sun-set-fraction step) focal camera-w)
       & (frame-ground w p.seg pose focal camera-w)
       & (spike-order w ch pose (collect w p.seg pose focal p.along spike-v 0.0) focal step 0)

Section: The Viewpoints

 Hand-picked spots on the route, each aimed at one thing worth seeing. The metres
 are route distance from the start; drive-dist-at divides by the same 0.995 the
 page uses, so these land where the page would if you drove to them.

 THE STILL USES A LEVEL CAMERA -- dead centre of the lane, no lean, no gaze --
 even though the page now runs the real rider and has all three. That is on
 purpose. A still is for reading the SCENERY, and a leaned camera rotates the
 whole frame and moves everything in it, which is exactly the confound you do not
 want when the question is where the herd is.

 They are also close in. The first set stood 90 to 470 m off, which put a 1.6 m
 zebra at about two pixels -- right at the cull floor, which is to say invisible.
 These stand 25 to 70 m short of what they are aimed at.

 THE BIG PIG HERD is a seven-by-seven block with jitter, placed 60 m before the
 end of a distraction segment. Segment 2 is the first of the two that carry one
 (49 pigs; every other segment has ten or none), it is 400 m long and starts at
 820 m, so the herd sits at about 1160 m and this stands 90 m short of it.

 A nominal speed, so the cat's crossing clock is live. A still with v = 0 freezes
 the cat at the start of its walk-in, which is the one pose it is never
 interesting in.

  spike-v : Real
  spike-v = 1.2

  spike-course : Real
  spike-course = course-length build-world * 0.995

  u-at-metres : Real -> Real
  u-at-metres (m) = m / spike-course

  u-pig-herd : Real
  u-pig-herd = u-at-metres 1130.0

 THE MID-TOWER. Segment 6 is the 1200 m leg, the only one long enough to stand a
 tower halfway down its own length rather than at an intersection. It starts at
 2120 m so the tower is at 2720, and this stands 140 m short.

  u-mid-tower : Real
  u-mid-tower = u-at-metres 2650.0

 THE DUCK POND at segment 12's exit corner, the one hand-drawn scene on the route:
 water, a mud bank, and three duck billboards floating on it. Segment 12 starts at
 5320 m and is 300 long, so the corner is at 5620 and this stands 70 m short.

  u-pond : Real
  u-pond = u-at-metres 5596.0

 A CORNER PAIR: segment 15's exit carries the zebras, an adult at the far outer
 corner and its baby beyond the turn. Segment 15 runs 6320 to 6620, so this stands
 20 m short of the corner. At the first attempt it stood 470 m off, which put a
 1.6 m zebra at about two pixels -- right on the cull floor, which is to say
 invisible, which is exactly what it looked like.

  u-zebras : Real
  u-zebras = u-at-metres 6600.0

 THE CAT, TWICE, because the interesting thing about it is a clock rather than a
 place. Segment 1 runs 500 to 820 and its cat waits at about 628 m -- world.zig
 rounds the desired 105 m up to just past a tree, so a tree stands between the
 rider and it. The first viewpoint catches it FROZEN in the lane, twenty-four
 frames of standing still; the second catches it AIRBORNE, mid-leap, which is the
 one place this port approximates and therefore the one worth looking at.

  u-cat-frozen : Real
  u-cat-frozen = u-at-metres 598.0

  u-cat-leap : Real
  u-cat-leap = u-at-metres 622.5

Section: The Speed Profile

 SPIKE ONLY, and this one draws no scenery at all -- it is a GRAPH.

 It exists because two different things were making the page move wrong and they
 could not be told apart by watching it: whether the rider's own accel and decel
 are right, and whether a frame is being shown at the right rate. This isolates
 the first completely. It runs the real ported physics -- `get-next-rider-state`,
 graded at 199 values one step from a shared state -- for as many frames as it
 takes to cover the route, and reports the speed and lean at each. No animation is
 involved, so nothing here can be blamed on the clock.

 Read it as: v should sit near v-max on the long straights, dip into every corner
 by the amount turn-speed allows for that turn's angle, and climb back out at
 a-accel. A flat line means nothing is braking. A sawtooth with the dips in the
 wrong places means the corner brake is reading the wrong segment.

  ProfilePt = record {
    dist : Real,
    v : Real,
    tilt : Real,
    seg : Integer
  }

 One step of the real rider, recorded. The route distance is what the graph's x
 axis wants and the state carries segment and along, so route-distance converts.

  profile-pt : List Segment, RiderState -> ProfilePt
  profile-pt (w) (s) =
    ProfilePt { dist = route-distance w s.segment s.along, v = s.v, tilt = s.tilt, seg = s.segment }

  profile-from : List Segment, RiderState, Integer -> List ProfilePt
  profile-from (w) (s) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then []
    else if is-finished s w then [profile-pt w s]
    else [profile-pt w s] & profile-from w (get-next-rider-state s w) (n - 1)

 Four thousand frames is comfortably more than the route takes at v-max, and the
 walk stops early at the finish line anyway.

  profile-frames : Integer
  profile-frames = 4000

  speed-profile : List ProfilePt
  speed-profile = profile-from build-world initial-rider-state profile-frames

Chapter: SpikeMain
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Sky
  cites Poc chapter Spike

 SPIKE ONLY. THROWAWAY. Prints one still frame's draw commands as text, for
 harness/spike_svg.py to turn into a picture. Nothing grades this and nothing
 depends on it.

 COORDINATES GO OUT AS SCALED INTEGERS because `show` on a Real is still refused
 by the zig plug -- it needs a __real_to_text it does not have. real-to-int landed
 in 2026-08-29's plug work, so hundredths of a pixel is a plain multiply and a
 truncation, and a hundredth of a pixel is far finer than anything an SVG viewed
 at 960 by 600 can show.

 ONE LINE FOR THE WHOLE FRAME, commands separated by semicolons, rather than a
 print per command. A Codex `act` has no recursive loop shape, and a chapter is
 not the place to discover one for a throwaway: building the text purely and
 handing it to a single print is the boring option and it is fine here, because a
 frame is about a hundred kilobytes of digits.

   C <tag> <colour> <strength e-3> <n> <x e-2> <y e-2> ... ; C ... ; C ...

 We say:

Section: Printing

  scaled : Real -> Text
  scaled (v) = show (real-to-int (v * 100.0))

  coords : List Real, Integer -> Text
  coords (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then ""
    else " " & scaled (list-at ps i) & coords ps (i + 1)

  cmd-line : DrawCmd -> Text
  cmd-line (c) =
    "C " & show c.tag & " " & show c.color & " " & show (real-to-int (c.strength * 1000.0)) & " " & show (list-length c.pts / 2) & coords c.pts 0

  all-cmds : List DrawCmd, Integer -> Text
  all-cmds (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then ""
    else cmd-line (list-at cs i) & " ; " & all-cmds cs (i + 1)

Section: Entry

 One block per viewpoint. The SCENE line carries the sky and horizon colours too,
 because blitter.js paints those itself from the module's numbers and an SVG has
 to do the same or the frame floats on nothing.

  scene-line : Text, Real -> Text
  scene-line (name) (u) =
    "SCENE " & name & " " & show (sky-color (scene-step-at u)) & " " & show (horizon-color (scene-step-at u))

  prof-pt : ProfilePt -> Text
  prof-pt (p) =
    show (real-to-int p.dist) & " " & show (real-to-int (p.v * 1000.0)) & " " & show (real-to-int (p.tilt * 1000.0)) & " " & show p.seg

  prof-all : List ProfilePt, Integer -> Text
  prof-all (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then ""
    else prof-pt (list-at ps i) & " ; " & prof-all ps (i + 1)

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("PROFILE " & show (list-length speed-profile))
    print-line-uni (prof-all speed-profile 0)
    print-line-uni (scene-line "pig-herd" u-pig-herd)
    print-line-uni (all-cmds (spike-frame-at u-pig-herd) 0)
    print-line-uni (scene-line "mid-tower" u-mid-tower)
    print-line-uni (all-cmds (spike-frame-at u-mid-tower) 0)
    print-line-uni (scene-line "duck-pond" u-pond)
    print-line-uni (all-cmds (spike-frame-at u-pond) 0)
    print-line-uni (scene-line "zebras" u-zebras)
    print-line-uni (all-cmds (spike-frame-at u-zebras) 0)
    print-line-uni (scene-line "cat-frozen" u-cat-frozen)
    print-line-uni (all-cmds (spike-frame-at u-cat-frozen) 0)
    print-line-uni (scene-line "cat-leap" u-cat-leap)
    print-line-uni (all-cmds (spike-frame-at u-cat-leap) 0)
  end
